Is "big, nasty spot" a
medical diagnosis these days?

00:09:42.990 --> 00:09:44.760
Yeah, I don't know what
that is and it's definitely

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:47.880
not life threatening in the
moment but in the jungle,

00:09:47.880 --> 00:09:50.400
I would love to know what
could have caused it.

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:52.150
This person obviously
knows way more than me

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:54.990
- And being close to
a joint like the knee,

00:09:54.990 --> 00:09:57.480
is that more serious?
- Correct.

00:09:57.480 --> 00:10:00.360
- Having an infection
near the knee puts you

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:02.040
at a higher risk for septic arthritis,

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:03.420
which is an infection of the joint

00:10:03.420 --> 00:10:05.700
or an osteomyelitis, which
is an infection of the bone.

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:08.400
Both of those are quite serious
and require IV antibiotics.

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:09.750
Is his wound that bad?

00:10:09.750 --> 00:10:11.610
The front one doesn't look terrible.,

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:13.290
It just looks like
there's some tissue there

00:10:13.290 --> 00:10:17.130
that's forming to create sort
of the next layer of healing.

00:10:17.130 --> 00:10:20.070
What you wanna look for is
the tissue surrounding it.

00:10:20.070 --> 00:10:21.930
Is the redness starting to spread?

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:23.520
Is there streaking happening?

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:26.460
Some fluctuance below the
wound, basically signs

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:28.560
of the cellulitis is
what you're looking for.

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:30.513
I didn't see that on that initial look.

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:33.510
Migraine?

00:10:33.510 --> 00:10:34.343
Nightmare?

00:10:37.590 --> 00:10:39.855
Back pain from sleeping on the floor?

00:10:39.855 --> 00:10:43.688
- I think it's an
emergency, it's really bad.

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:47.850
- Bruce had really horrible stomach pains.

00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:50.160
He's had pains for about four days now

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:54.060
and it just got really unbearable for him.

00:10:54.060 --> 00:10:55.950
- The worry here is appendicitis.

00:10:55.950 --> 00:10:57.750
Obviously that's a surgical emergency.

00:10:57.750 --> 00:10:59.190
Colitis would be a big problem too.

00:10:59.190 --> 00:11:00.754
You're gonna need antibiotics for that.

00:11:00.754 --> 00:11:03.325
- [Medic] And I'm gonna
assess you first before I can

00:11:03.325 --> 00:11:04.740
start giving you any treatment.

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:06.330
- That's really important
with abdominal pain,

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:09.180
that you don't give
pain medicine too early

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:10.890
in the treatment before you examine

00:11:10.890 --> 00:11:12.925
because you might not
know what's going on.

00:11:12.925 --> 00:11:15.600
- [Man] Don't, don't, don't.

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:18.750
- The areas where he's pushing

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:20.340
looks like it's an appendicitis.

00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:23.490
Technically appendicitis starts
on the right lower quadrant

00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:25.530
but there's been stories
of patients having it

00:11:25.530 --> 00:11:26.700
in the center of their abdomen,

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:28.530
then migrating to the
right lower quadrant.

00:11:28.530 --> 00:11:31.710
Some people having contralateral
pain on the opposite side.

00:11:31.710 --> 00:11:34.920
So there's never a clear
answer with appendicitis.

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:37.290
Especially, it depends on where in

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:39.210
the course of the
appendicitis that you are.

00:11:39.210 --> 00:11:41.790
Early stages, late
stages, has it perforated,

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:43.470
now it's spreading through
your entire abdomen?

00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:45.450
- [Medic] I can't rule out
that it's not his appendix.

00:11:45.450 --> 00:11:46.283
- [Man] Sure.
- Point is,

00:11:46.283 --> 00:11:48.420
we're gonna take him.

00:11:48.420 --> 00:11:51.150
- Getting transported like
that with an appendicitis

00:11:51.150 --> 00:11:53.880
would be so painful because
any kind of jiggling

00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:57.150
of the abdomen, of an acute
abdomen, is so painful.

00:11:57.150 --> 00:11:58.680
That's why one of the
special tests we use is

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:00.810
we have a patient jump
on their heels to see

00:12:00.810 --> 00:12:02.610
if there is a truly acute abdomen.

00:12:02.610 --> 00:12:03.443
They'll tell you right away,

00:12:03.443 --> 00:12:04.950
they'll scream and tell you it's terrible.

00:12:04.950 --> 00:12:06.480
- They ran some tests and found out

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:08.460
that his entire digestive system,

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:10.825
his colon and his bladder were blocked.

00:12:10.825 --> 00:12:12.060
Nothing was coming out.

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:13.456
- What?

00:12:13.456 --> 00:12:14.850
Are they saying he was constipated?

00:12:14.850 --> 00:12:16.110
Something doesn't make sense.

00:12:16.110 --> 00:12:17.880
He had such bad constipation

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:19.923
that it was also blocking his bladder?

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:23.730
Because the colon runs from here, across,

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:27.540
up and then down and then
becomes the small intestine.

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:29.730
The bladder's down here in the pubic area.

00:12:29.730 --> 00:12:30.680
The only thing that it could be,

00:12:30.680 --> 00:12:34.830
a (stutters) vesiculocolinic fistula.

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:37.530
Where if you have a passageway in between,

00:12:37.530 --> 00:12:40.290
created between the colon and the bladder,

00:12:40.290 --> 00:12:42.360
where feces can actually
travel into the bladder,

00:12:42.360 --> 00:12:43.903
causing an infection and a blockage.

00:12:43.903 --> 00:12:45.687
- I can't feel my hands.

00:12:45.687 --> 00:12:47.220
- Why can't she feel her hands?

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:48.240
- All right, you're all
right, you're all right.

00:12:48.240 --> 00:12:49.560
I'm right here with you.

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:50.393
- You doing all right?

00:12:50.393 --> 00:12:52.620
- I can't tell if I'm
panicking or dehydrated.

00:12:52.620 --> 00:12:54.060
- Hey medical, Dr. Joe.

00:12:54.060 --> 00:12:56.389
- Dr. Joe, Dr. Mike,
we're all first name here.

00:12:56.389 --> 00:12:58.350
- My arms are cramping up.

00:12:58.350 --> 00:12:59.308
- [Medic] That's because you're breathing

00:12:59.308 --> 00:13:00.390
a little bit quickly, all right?

00:13:00.390 --> 00:13:01.443
- What's wrong with Hannah?

00:13:01.443 --> 00:13:02.312
- I don't know.

00:13:02.312 --> 00:13:03.660
- You're not gonna let me die?

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:04.577
- Of course not.

00:13:04.577 --> 00:13:06.000
- (laughs) "You're not gonna let me die."

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:07.170
I love that, he's God.

00:13:07.170 --> 00:13:09.240
He controls who lives and dies.

00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:10.710
- [Medic] This is just
because you're breathing

00:13:10.710 --> 00:13:11.580
a little bit quick, Hannah.

00:13:11.580 --> 00:13:13.020
- So if you breathe out too quickly,

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:17.026
you can actually create an
oxygen carbon dioxide imbalance,

00:13:17.026 --> 00:13:20.820
sort of over blow off carbon
dioxide and as a result,

00:13:20.820 --> 00:13:23.880
cause an acid base imbalance
in your bloodstream.

00:13:23.880 --> 00:13:26.010
- I knew coming out
here that panic attacks

00:13:26.010 --> 00:13:27.870
are something that I'm prone to

00:13:27.870 --> 00:13:29.880
but I just didn't wanna
be one of those people.

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:33.000
- Well, panic attacks can
make the situation worse

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:34.890
because during a panic attack,

00:13:34.890 --> 00:13:36.330
you feel tightness of the chest,

00:13:36.330 --> 00:13:37.620
you start getting anxious.

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:38.820
When you're hyperventilating,

00:13:38.820 --> 00:13:40.950
you release too much carbon dioxide

00:13:40.950 --> 00:13:42.270
and when you release carbon dioxide,

00:13:42.270 --> 00:13:43.830
which acts as an acid in your blood,

00:13:43.830 --> 00:13:46.107
you actually shift that
to have your blood become

00:13:46.107 --> 00:13:48.570
a higher pH, which makes it more basic.

00:13:48.570 --> 00:13:50.850
That's why people say breathe into a bag

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:53.310
because then you re-inhale
some of that carbon dioxide.

00:13:53.310 --> 00:13:54.300
- [Sam] Oh, really?

00:13:54.300 --> 00:13:55.650
That's where the bag thing is from?

00:13:55.650 --> 00:13:56.670
- Yeah.

